#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068624 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0e5b503-a393-58ef-bbcd-5c6d65257f53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "bd57c7f2-87b5-55a0-9237-1ef814f59898" :question/math-academy-id "e-6136" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $2x < 10\\,?$\n\nI. $x=0$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 0 & <10 \\\\ 0 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=0$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=0$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & <10 \\\\ 4 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}5}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & <10 \\\\ 10 & <10\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=5$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=5$ **does not satisfy** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068625 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09fdde75-121e-5378-bad1-1ab564375d41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Compound Inequality"}] :question/id #uuid "74587421-d1ad-550e-a30d-4d4768588d4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6137" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $1 \\leq \\dfrac{x}{2}-1 < 3\\,?$\n\nI. $x=0$\n\nII. $x=4$\n\nIII. $x=10$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{0}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 0-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le -1<3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=0$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=0$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}4}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{4}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 1<3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}10}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{10}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 5-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 4<3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=10$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=10$ **does not satisfy** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd07be8d-2eba-5e4e-ba42-72470fd12120" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inequalities that are Satisfied by a Given Value"}] :question/id #uuid "fb564b00-2d04-556a-a662-7b92e21ec3d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6138" :question/problem "The value $a = 2$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{a}{2} + 2 > 5$\n\nII. $1 \\leq 2a < 6$\n\nIII. $3a - 1 < 10$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}2}$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{2}+2 & >5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{2}+2 & >5 \\\\ 1+2 & >5 \\\\ 3 & >5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a = 2$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le 2a<6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2\\cdot a<6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2\\cdot 2<6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 4<6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a = 2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}2}$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-1 & <10 \\\\ 3\\cdot a-1 & <10 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2-1 & <10 \\\\ 5 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a = 2$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c472f18-da99-5969-acdf-1a7b53465494" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality with Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "73bc0b43-f3a9-5a4b-a49f-833cdf1f493f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6139" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{2t}{3} < 8\\,?$\n\nI. $t=9$\n\nII. $t=6$\n\nIII. $t=3$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}9}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2t}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot t}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 9}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & <8 \\\\ 6 & <8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t = 9$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=9$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}6}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2t}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot t}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 6}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{12}{3} & <8 \\\\ 4 & <8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=6$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}t}={\\color{blue}3}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2t}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot t}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 3}{3} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & <8 \\\\ 2 & <8\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=3$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=3$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0e5b503-a393-58ef-bbcd-5c6d65257f53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a13b6b6-fb2a-5334-a7bf-3fff7c540864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483638} {:answer/id #uuid "af1515f1-8703-5e26-bbaf-816dfadc04ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483918} {:answer/id #uuid "bdaf4242-0292-5b55-bd2a-653f3f21dd42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483944} {:answer/id #uuid "cc59a325-1994-55cf-a969-91903d7415d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483976} {:answer/id #uuid "f5ec1838-7945-58f3-a872-5be065f9f966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdaf4242-0292-5b55-bd2a-653f3f21dd42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483944} :answer-field/id #uuid "53609486-74ae-5701-96b5-63b48ffe168e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba00c538-8a3f-5eb5-9ed3-b95b47d4f897" :question/math-academy-id "q-101896" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $7t\\ge 19\\,?$\n\nI. $t=2$\n\nII. $t=3$\n\nIII. $t=4$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=2$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7t & \\ge 19 \\\\ 7\\cdot 2 & \\ge 19 \\\\ 14 & \\ge 19\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=2$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=2$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=3$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7t & \\ge 19 \\\\ 7\\cdot 3 & \\ge 19 \\\\ 21 & \\ge 19\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=3$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=3$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=4$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7t & \\ge 19 \\\\ 7\\cdot 4 & \\ge 19 \\\\ 28 & \\ge 19\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=4$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=4$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c472f18-da99-5969-acdf-1a7b53465494" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality with Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e3ddfa-f6a3-5929-bc7b-3fdafb723dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483626} {:answer/id #uuid "366d91d6-ce40-520f-94a7-6a25b93c8dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483665} {:answer/id #uuid "6144a317-d27b-5494-b256-1910d5d5a596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483753} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5985b7-1403-5272-9da0-779b45b940ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483860} {:answer/id #uuid "9dad366a-4f7d-5b18-a2d4-c7fa37842123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6144a317-d27b-5494-b256-1910d5d5a596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483753} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e7a76e-0709-521a-acee-f4577fe388b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ec4ef84-8b35-542a-b22f-98607c9a4fdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-101902" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $t$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{5t}{2}\\ge 15\\,?$\n\nI. $t=4$\n\nII. $t=6$\n\nIII. $t=8$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=4$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 4}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{20}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 10 & \\ge 15\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=4$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=4$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=6$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 6}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{30}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 15 & \\ge 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=6$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=6$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=8$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot 8}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ \\frac{40}{2} & \\ge 15 \\\\ 20 & \\ge 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=8$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=8$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068783 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09fdde75-121e-5378-bad1-1ab564375d41" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Compound Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10435875-4846-5e55-a9e8-4d80cac5281b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483594} {:answer/id #uuid "7db63552-5c7d-5c68-ac11-7a7ce1b6de39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483822} {:answer/id #uuid "983ce44a-5bdf-586e-b6d5-a3864329b256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483881} {:answer/id #uuid "b69f2e39-01dd-5e02-aa4d-c589f3591e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483933} {:answer/id #uuid "f15a9139-eccc-5011-b1d2-60c3b89344ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b69f2e39-01dd-5e02-aa4d-c589f3591e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483933} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d916bac-35e5-5881-bc31-39a6abe4460e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54c177e7-bfd3-5330-bd52-c67fa05a6194" :question/math-academy-id "q-101911" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $w$ satisfy the inequality $2\\le 5w\\le 15\\,?$\n\nI. $w=1$\n\nII. $w=2$\n\nIII. $w=3$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=1$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le 5w & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5\\cdot 1 & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5 & \\le 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=1$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=1$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=2$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le 5w & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5\\cdot 2 & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 10 & \\le 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $w=3$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le 5w & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 5\\cdot 3 & \\le 15 \\\\ 2\\le 15 & \\le 15\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $w=3$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $w=3$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd07be8d-2eba-5e4e-ba42-72470fd12120" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inequalities that are Satisfied by a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "514f8922-b347-5668-a2b6-3f0332a95bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483714} {:answer/id #uuid "69129fee-bad0-5e1b-9cd9-41589471df8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483772} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8fc44f-d031-5b75-9ae4-fa7600e11c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483773} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b7b5b1-8220-55ae-803d-96a0641b4b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483991} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e579de-76fa-5260-86cc-b3e690ef1105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "514f8922-b347-5668-a2b6-3f0332a95bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483714} :answer-field/id #uuid "496a2473-4935-5c87-839f-9e31b7c396d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b784d723-3b79-553d-9de5-8ee50049511b" :question/math-academy-id "q-101918" :question/problem "The value $x=4$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $x-3>0$\n\nII. $2x\\le 10$\n\nIII. $1<x+5<6$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & >0 \\\\ 4-3 & >0 \\\\ 1 & >0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x & \\le 10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & \\le 10 \\\\ 8 & \\le 10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x+5 & <6 \\\\ 1<4+5 & <6 \\\\ 1<9 & <6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd07be8d-2eba-5e4e-ba42-72470fd12120" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inequalities that are Satisfied by a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e75d4a6-2669-5c44-ac92-fa2be3b0445b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483684} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9e81a2-f31e-55b9-a5e6-70b006029fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483741} {:answer/id #uuid "980f6b23-f70a-5b68-b1aa-6ca551113ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483879} {:answer/id #uuid "b5598001-4854-5d70-b2e9-4dd1fddd4779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483928} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7b66bd-f726-5f5c-91b1-bf4e2a07c0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "980f6b23-f70a-5b68-b1aa-6ca551113ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483879} :answer-field/id #uuid "292093ae-ac58-50d6-9dd4-5ef1e35ec2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "516935e4-c5da-561b-9d27-f7806d4435cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-101919" :question/problem "The value $t=-3$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $0\\le t-4<7$\n\nII. $t+2>-5$\n\nIII. $\\frac{t}{3}>-3$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=-3$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0\\le t-4 & <7 \\\\ 0\\le -3-4 & <7 \\\\ 0\\le -7 & <7\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=-3$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=-3$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=-3$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t+2 & >-5 \\\\ -3+2 & >-5 \\\\ -1 & >-5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=-3$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=-3$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $t=-3$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{t}{3} & >-3 \\\\ \\frac{-3}{3} & >-3 \\\\ -1 & >-3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $t=-3$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $t=-3$ **satisfies** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd07be8d-2eba-5e4e-ba42-72470fd12120" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inequalities that are Satisfied by a Given Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf9db0b-3e02-53ef-a718-bd9f4cc10507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483707} {:answer/id #uuid "5e09a1c4-8610-517a-b7d1-71fa26a84d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483747} {:answer/id #uuid "7719616d-8392-5fb6-81c4-1181902f9b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483807} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b24ee0-2b5e-5755-84df-e459e1092c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483911} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8cfc46-b6b6-548b-9238-ec1864855028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb8cfc46-b6b6-548b-9238-ec1864855028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483973} :answer-field/id #uuid "6262fb17-ee28-540a-b676-329c7867b0a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cad781ca-8e12-5b8d-a5a5-787d8ffa6e99" :question/math-academy-id "q-101923" :question/problem "The value $z=0$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $z+3\\ge 1$\n\nII. $3z+1<0$\n\nIII. $-6\\le \\frac{z}{2}-1\\le -2$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $z=0$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+3 & \\ge 1 \\\\ 0+3 & \\ge 1 \\\\ 3 & \\ge 1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $z=0$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $z=0$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $z=0$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+1 & <0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 0+1 & <0 \\\\ 1 & <0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $z=0$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $z=0$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $z=0$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\le \\frac{z}{2}-1 & \\le -2 \\\\ -6\\le \\frac{0}{2}-1 & \\le -2 \\\\ -6\\le -1 & \\le -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $z=0$ leads to a **false** statement.  \n\nTherefore, $z=0$ **does not satisfy** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c472f18-da99-5969-acdf-1a7b53465494" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality with Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c95956d-2dc0-5f7c-979f-cf8f22837dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483614} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9b87ed-1120-58e9-9e0d-b9a8766d6135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483855} {:answer/id #uuid "c99a01a0-a208-5ce6-bbaa-021cf7c9907d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483966} {:answer/id #uuid "d9977d7f-d937-5e30-a6f8-a1017cc6d629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483999} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cafb9d-01be-54ae-b97a-cdbdbf2750b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b9b87ed-1120-58e9-9e0d-b9a8766d6135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483855} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d9b0107-b569-53ee-8857-5d85df2f3143" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44ec8528-0626-53ff-b6b0-f0595765d80d" :question/math-academy-id "q-63127" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $a$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{a}{2}<10\\,?$\n\nI. $a=10$\n\nII. $a=14$\n\nIII. $a=20$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $a=10$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{2} & <10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{2} & <10 \\\\ 5 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a=10$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a=10$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $a=14$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{2} & <10 \\\\ \\frac{14}{2} & <10 \\\\ 7 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a=14$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a=14$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $a=20$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{2} & <10 \\\\ \\frac{20}{2} & <10 \\\\ 10 & <10\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a=20$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a=20$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0e5b503-a393-58ef-bbcd-5c6d65257f53" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that Satisfy a Given Inequality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11aaec88-b13f-5b4d-a3bd-877b9f646994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483596} {:answer/id #uuid "210737d7-92bd-53d4-861b-0ea78b2bde49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483622} {:answer/id #uuid "89d404d2-87db-54c4-92cc-4f36618a3674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483851} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0d7e96-587e-51d8-a555-42603856d0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484006} {:answer/id #uuid "f66c24b8-1c56-5e40-8330-d98458731c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89d404d2-87db-54c4-92cc-4f36618a3674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483851} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad86c17-9879-591e-829a-925730f4a151" :answer-field/key 